# Xenith — the dark theme, measured against the reading research
#
# Catppuccin Mocha, kept where it already meets the evidence and changed
# where it does not. A whole-system theme like the others; tools/xen
# also pins a stand-alone Xenith to it. The xenith-* keys at the end set
# Xenith's colours by name, so nothing is left to the role mapping.
#
# The rules, and where they come from (docs/THEME-RESEARCH.md has the
# evidence and the full references):
#  - Readability comes from luminance contrast, not hue (Legge 1990;
#    Knoblauch 1991), and it levels off well short of the maximum
#    (Legge, Rubin & Luebker 1987). So: dark grey, not black, behind
#    off-white, not white. Mocha's body already does this: background
#    L* 12, text L* 86, 11.3:1, APCA Lc 80.
#  - Desaturated colours read as well as any (Pastoor 1990). Colours
#    that are read as text are the paler, less saturated ones, at 7:1 or
#    better; no saturated blue for thin text.
#  - Contrast is measured with APCA as well as WCAG 2, whose ratio
#    misjudges pairs near black. Body text is held to Lc 75 or better.
#  - A selection need only show as an area (Lc 15 against the body);
#    the text on it is held to the body's standard, so selected text is
#    drawn brighter rather than the fill made darker. Xenith's three
#    selections (buttons 1, 2, 3) share one lightness and differ in
#    hue; which button made one is known without its colour.
#  - No syntax colouring: its effect on reading code is nil or small
#    (Hannebauer et al. 2018).
#
# Mocha's colours that failed and what replaced them:
#  dim 6C7086 (3.4:1) -> A6ADC8, accent 89B4FA (Lc 59) -> B4BEFE,
#  red F38BA8 (Lc 55) -> EBA0AC, menu selection 585B70 (text 4.6:1) ->
#  45475A, Xenith's button 2 and 3 fills (pastel, glaring in a dark
#  page) -> muted fills at the selection's lightness.

